.\" The manual page of aqr, in mdoc(7). Every option of `aqr --help` is here: a test of
.\" crates/aqr (`man_page_has_every_option`) checks it.
.Dd November 9, 2026
.Dt AQR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aqr ,
.Nm unaqr
.Nd archive files with lossless recompression
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar input
.Op Fl -rename Ar to
.Ar ...
.Nm
.Fl d
.Op Ar options
.Ar archive
.Op Ar path Op Fl -rename Ar to
.Ar ...
.Nm
.Fl d
.Fl -each
.Op Ar options
.Ar archive ...
.Nm
.Fl t
.Op Fl q | v
.Op Fl -json
.Ar archive
.Op Ar path ...
.Nm
.Fl K
.Op Ar options
.Ar archive ...
.Nm
.Fl I
.Op Fl v
.Ar archive
.Op Ar path ...
.Nm
.Fl -delete
.Op Fl -compact
.Op Fl q
.Ar archive
.Ar path ...
.Nm
.Fl -move
.Op Fl q | v
.Ar archive
.Ar path
.Fl -rename Ar to
.Ar ...
.Nm
.Fl -compact
.Op Fl q | v
.Ar archive
.Nm unaqr
.Op Ar options
.Ar archive
.Op Ar path ...
.Nm
.Fl -setup-autocomplete Ns Op = Ns Ar shell
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes Aqueous archives: files and directories with their metadata, the compressed data
in them (DEFLATE streams, JPEG, MP3, FLAC, PDF, ZIP, LZMA, executables and more)
recompressed losslessly, so that each file comes back byte for byte.
An archive carries the WebAssembly modules that decode it; known ones run natively,
others in a sandbox.
.Pp
By default, each
.Ar input
is compressed into
.Ar input Ns .aqr :
a file as an archive of a single file, a directory as an archive of what it holds.
With
.Fl o ,
all the inputs go into one archive, several of them by their paths: a relative one as
given, an absolute one or one with
.Pa ..
by its last name.
Run as
.Nm unaqr ,
it decompresses by default.
.Pp
.Nm
also reads ZIP, 7z, RAR, ALZ, EGG and tar archives (alone or under gzip, bzip2, xz,
LZMA or Zstandard) and those compressors' single files, extracting them as
.Xr tar 1 ,
.Xr unzip 1
and 7-Zip do; and writes ZIP, 7z, ALZ, EGG and tar archives
.Pq Fl -format .
.Ss Modes
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl -compress
Compress (the default as
.Nm ) .
.It Fl d , Fl -decompress
Extract each archive into its name without
.Pa .aqr ,
or
.Fl o .
An archive of another format is extracted as it is into the current directory (or
.Fl o ) ,
a compressor's single file into its name without its extension.
Given
.Ar path Ns s
after the archive, those alone are extracted (a directory with what is under it), where
the whole archive would have them; only what they need is decoded.
.It Fl t , Fl -list
List the entries of each archive, or those at its
.Ar path Ns s ,
with their sizes, modification times, flags and how each file is made.
.Fl q
lists the paths alone,
.Fl v
the blocks each file takes, its formula and all its metadata.
.It Fl K , Fl -test
Decode and check each archive, writing nothing; tell how far it conforms to the archival
profile where it has mirror blocks (or with
.Fl v ) .
.It Fl I , Fl -info
Tell all that an archive records, for debugging: its blocks and segments, or of each
.Ar path
its entry in every segment with its formula in full.
.It Fl -delete
Take the
.Ar path Ns s
given after the archive (a directory with what is under it) out of it, in place: a
segment saying so is appended and committed, as
.Fl a
appends, so that the file holds the archive as it was or as edited whatever happens
meanwhile.
The bytes of what was taken out stay in the file (but with
.Fl -compact ) .
A path not in the archive is an error, before anything is written; an encrypted
archive is not edited.
.It Fl -move
Move each
.Ar path
of the archive given after it (a directory with what is under it) to the
.Ar to
of the
.Fl -rename
after it, in the order given, making the directories on the way; the archive is then
written again as
.Fl -compact
writes it, in place.
A
.Ar to
there already, under the path moved or under what is not a directory is an error, before
anything is written.
.It Fl -keep-since Ar spec
With
.Fl -compact
of an archive with journal points
.Pq Fl j :
keep those from the one
.Ar spec
picks
.Pq as Fl -at
on, with the states between them, and write what is before as one state: the segments before
the point as the state at it, the others as they are.
.It Fl -drop-history
With
.Fl -compact :
keep no journal point, and none of the states between.
An archive with points is not written again without one of the two.
.It Fl -compact
Write an archive again of what it holds, segment by segment, copying the blocks it still
needs as they are (those shared with what is gone made again of what is left): what
.Fl a ,
.Fl s
and
.Fl -delete
replaced or took out leaves the file.
It is written beside the archive, checked, and put in its place by a rename, so that the
file holds the archive as it was or as written again whatever happens meanwhile; bytes
before the archive (a stub) are kept.
With
.Fl -delete ,
the paths are taken out as it is written.
Archives of mirror blocks
.Pq Fl A
are not written again yet.
.Fl v
tells the blocks left out.
.Fl v
gives the unknown metadata uncut.
.El
.Ss Inputs
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -rename Ar to
After an input, archive it at
.Ar to
.Pq names between Ql / ;
after a
.Ar path
of an archive, extract it there (with
.Fl -move ,
move it there).
.It Fl x Ar name , Fl -exclude Ar name
Leave out the entries named
.Ar name
wherever they are found, or, where
.Ar name
has a
.Ql /
but at its end, the entry at that path in the archive; with what is under them.
Matched as given: there are no wildcards yet.
May be given more than once.
.It Fl -files-from Ar file
Take the inputs (decompressing, the
.Ar path Ns s
of the archive) listed in
.Ar file
.Po
.Ql -
for standard input
.Pc ,
a line each, where the option is given.
.It Fl -null
The lists of
.Fl -files-from
after it have each path ended by NUL, not by a line's end.
.It Fl L , Fl -dereference
Follow links, given or found, archiving what they lead to; a link leading nowhere or to a
directory it is in is kept as a link.
.It Fl C Ar dir , Fl -directory Ar dir
Compressing, find the inputs after it in
.Ar dir
and archive them as if run there; decompressing, extract into
.Ar dir .
The archives' own paths are taken from where
.Nm
is run.
.It Fl -each
Take every input as an archive, each extracted (or listed) whole.
.It Fl
As an input, standard input: compressing, the only input, read whole and archived as a
single file; decompressing, an Aqueous archive (read whole first).
Its output is standard output unless
.Fl o
is given.
Where no input is given and standard input is not a terminal, it is the input (but with
.Fl t
and
.Fl I ) .
.El
.Ss Output
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar output , Fl -output Ar output
Write to
.Ar output ;
compressing, all the inputs into one archive.
.Fl o Fl
is standard output: compressing, the archive (never to a terminal, but with
.Fl f ) ,
an archive of several files written as it is made; decompressing, the file of an archive
of a single file, or the one file of the
.Ar path Ns s
given.
.It Fl -auto-dir
Decompressing, extract a single entry at the top of an archive beside it, else into a
directory of the archive's name without its extension beside it;
.Fl o
is where it goes instead of beside it.
.It Fl f , Fl -force
Overwrite outputs.
.It Fl -rm
Remove the inputs once archived (and checked), or an archive once extracted whole;
what changed meanwhile is kept and told.
.It Fl p , Fl -preserve
Keep and apply the metadata not commonly kept too: access, status change and backup
times; extracting, ownership, flags only the superuser can change, set-user-ID and
set-group-ID bits, FIFOs and devices.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Log nothing but errors.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Log each file archived: what it was taken for, the modules its formula applies and the
bytes of the archive that are its.
.It Fl -stats Ns Op = Ns Ar file
Tell, after each archive, what stands out of where the time went;
.Ar file Ns .json
or
.Ar file Ns .html
writes all that was sampled.
May be given more than once.
.It Fl -json
With
.Fl t ,
write a line of JSON for each archive: its entries with all their metadata, and the
counts and totals.
.It Fl -ipc
Be driven by a graphical frontend through standard input and output.
.El
.Ss Compression
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl P , Fl -passphrase
Encrypt the archives with a passphrase asked for on the terminal (twice).
Decompressing, ask for it at once; an encrypted archive asks for one anyway where there is
a terminal.
Without a terminal, a line of standard input is taken.
.It Fl -passphrase-from Ar source
As
.Fl P ,
the passphrase taken from
.Ar source
and nothing asked for:
.Cm stdin ,
a line of standard input;
.Cm fd: Ns Ar n ,
a line of file descriptor
.Ar n ;
.Cm env: Ns Ar name ,
the value of environment variable
.Ar name ;
any other path, the first line of that file
.Po
.Pa ./env:x
for a file named so
.Pc .
.It Fl l Ar level , Fl -level Ar level , Fl 0 No ... Fl 9
The level of compression:
.Cm 0
stores the data uncompressed,
.Cm 1
compresses it with zstd alone,
.Cm 2
to
.Cm 9
recompress it the more;
.Cm low ,
.Cm mid
(the default) and
.Cm high
are 2, 5 and 8.
.Cm zstd Ns Ar n
is zstd level
.Ar n
with the recompression of
.Cm mid .
With
.Fl -format ,
the format's level.
.It Fl -format Ar format
Write archives of another format:
.Cm zip , 7z , alz , egg , tar , tar.gz , tar.bz2 , tar.xz , tar.lzma , tar.zst ,
or
.Cm gz , bz2 , xz , lzma , zst
of a single file;
.Cm aqr
is the default.
Also chosen by the extension of
.Fl o Ns 's
name.
.It Fl -depth Ar n
Recompress formats nested at most
.Ar n
deep (default 3).
.It Fl B Ar size , Fl -batch Ar size
Make about
.Ar size
of files at once (default 512M), which bounds the memory compression takes.
.It Fl b Ar size , Fl -block-size Ar size
The most bytes of a block that files share (default 16M): smaller for faster random
access.
.It Fl S Ar size , Fl -segment-size Ar size
Close a segment of the archive after about
.Ar size
of it (default 256M;
.Cm 0 ,
one segment), so that an archive cut short or damaged loses no more than a segment's
names.
.It Fl D , Fl -dictionaries
Compress blocks alike with dictionaries trained for them where it pays.
.It Fl A , Fl -archival
Make an archive for long-term preservation: the blocks whose damage would lose more than a
file mirrored, to be restored from as it is read.
.It Fl -no-check
Do not decode the archive made to check it.
.It Fl -atomic
Make the archive whole or not at all.
By default an archive of several files is committed as it is written, so that a stopped
run (Ctrl-C) leaves an archive of what was written.
.It Fl a , Fl -append
Append the inputs to an archive (the first input, or
.Fl o ) :
what is not in it, or changed by size and modification time.
.It Fl s , Fl -sync
As
.Fl a ,
and take away what the archive holds that the inputs do not.
.It Fl j , Fl -journal Ar label
With
.Fl c ,
.Fl a
or
.Fl s :
name the state the archive is left in, with the time, as a journal point to return to
.Pq Fl -at ,
also where the run changed nothing (a segment of no entries then carries the point).
.Ql {today}
in the label is the local date,
.Ql {now}
the local date and time;
.Ql {{
and
.Ql }}
are braces.
A run stopped before it ended names nothing.
.El
.Ss Decompression
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -at Ar spec
With
.Fl t ,
.Fl d
or
.Fl K :
the archive as it was at a journal point.
.Ar spec
is
.Cm # Ns Ar N
(the Nth point from 1;
.Cm #- Ns Ar N
from the last),
.Cm @ Ns Ar label
(the last point of the label), or a date,
.Ar YYYY Ns Op - Ns Ar MM Ns Op - Ns Ar DD Ns Op T Ns Ar HH Ns Op : Ns Ar MM Ns Op : Ns Ar SS ,
in local time or followed by
.Cm Z
or an offset
.Cm +HH:MM :
the last point at or before the end of the period it names (2026-11-01 is up to the end of that day).
.It Fl -history
List the journal points of the archives: their numbers, times and labels.
.It Fl E Ar enc , Fl -encoding Ar enc
How names of no declared encoding are taken:
.Cm auto
(the default: as bytes where this system takes them, else decoded from a set guessed),
.Cm raw
(as bytes),
.Cm system ,
or sets such as
.Cm shift_jis , euc-kr , gbk , big5 , cp437
(several: guessed among them).
.It Fl -normalize Ar form
.Cm auto
(the default: composed, but on macOS),
.Cm nfc ,
.Cm nfd
or
.Cm keep .
.It Fl -bad-names Ar what
Names this system cannot write as they are:
.Cm auto
(the default),
.Cm keep
(as bytes, where the system takes them),
.Cm replace
.Po
.Ql %XX ,
U+FFFD,
.Ql _
.Pc
or
.Cm fail .
.It Fl -links Ar what
Which links are made:
.Cm inside
(the default: those whose targets stay in the directory extracted into; the others told),
or
.Cm all .
.It Fl -wasm
Run every module as WebAssembly, the known ones too.
.It Fl -no-sandbox
Run the WebAssembly modules in this process, not in worker processes locked down by the
system; where the system cannot lock workers down, they run only so.
.El
.Ss General
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl T Ar n , Fl -threads Ar n
Threads to use (default 0: as many as the machine has).
.It Fl M Ar mem , Fl -memory Ar mem
About the most heap to take at once: a
.Ar size ,
.Ar n Ns %
of the system's memory,
.Cm avail
(most of what is available as it starts), or
.Cm 0
(no limit); by default 2 GB, or half the system's if less.
It also bounds the memory of the modules run as WebAssembly.
.It Fl -application-bound Ar size
The most memory an application of a module may need.
Compressing (default 256M, or
.Cm unlimited ) ,
recompressions needing more are not used; decompressing (default 512M, which every
decoder supports), an archive needing more fails.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show a summary of the options.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Show the version.
.It Fl -setup-autocomplete Ns Op = Ns Ar shell
Given alone: set up the completion of
.Nm Ns 's
and
.Nm unaqr Ns 's
options and their values for
.Ar shell :
.Cm zsh , bash , fish
or
.Cm powershell ;
by default that of
.Ev SHELL ,
PowerShell on Windows.
The script is written into
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/aqr
(else
.Pa ~/.local/share/aqr ;
.Pa %LOCALAPPDATA%\eAqueous
on Windows) and a line reading it added to
.Pa ~/.zshrc ,
.Pa ~/.bashrc
or PowerShell's profile where it is not there yet; fish's go into
.Pa ~/.config/fish/completions .
.El
.Pp
A
.Ar size
is a number of bytes, or of KiB, MiB or GiB with
.Ql K ,
.Ql M
or
.Ql G .
Short options may be bundled
.Pq Fl cf , Fl T8 , Fl oOUT ;
a long option's value may follow it after
.Ql =
.Pq Fl -memory=2G .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where standard input is read into
.Pq Fl ,
and an archive for standard output is made where it is not written as it is made
.Pq Fl o Fl .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
Failed otherwise: reading or writing files, an archive of another format, ...
.It 2
The arguments are wrong.
.It 3
An archive is invalid: damaged or cut short, files lost to it (repaired or not).
.It 4
An archive is not decrypted: no passphrase given opens it.
.It 5
An archive needs more than this decoder supports or is allowed
.Pq Fl M , Fl -application-bound .
.It 6
An archive has a critical extension not known.
.It 7
Done but for what was told left out: entries not extracted, inputs not removed
.Pq Fl -rm .
.It 130
Stopped (Ctrl-C, a signal, the frontend).
.El
.Pp
Where several apply, the most severe (in the order 7, 3, 4, 5, 6) is the exit status.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Archive a directory into
.Pa photos.aqr ,
and extract it again:
.Dl $ aqr photos
.Dl $ aqr -d photos.aqr
.Pp
Archive two inputs into one archive at the highest level, leaving out
.Pa .git
directories:
.Dl $ aqr -9 -x .git -o work.aqr src docs
.Pp
Extract one file of an archive to standard output:
.Dl $ aqr -d -o - work.aqr src/main.rs | less
.Pp
Archive on another machine and extract here:
.Dl $ ssh host 'aqr -o - project' | aqr -d - -o project
.Pp
Encrypt with a passphrase of a file, then list the archive as JSON:
.Dl $ aqr --passphrase-from=key.txt -o secret.aqr secret
.Dl $ aqr -t --json --passphrase-from=key.txt secret.aqr | jq .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr 7z 1 ,
.Xr tar 1 ,
.Xr unzip 1 ,
.Xr zstd 1
.Pp
The Aqueous format specification.
